---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Restraint protects meaning" currentPath="/manifesto/foundations/restraint">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Entry</a> / <a href="/manifesto">Manifesto</a> / <a href="/manifesto/foundations">Foundations</a> / Restraint
  </div>

  <article class="principle-document">
    <header class="principle-header">
      <div class="principle-meta">
        <span class="principle-number">I.1</span>
        <span class="plate-id">E01-P03</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Restraint protects meaning</h1>
      <p class="abstract">Meaning collapses when too many signals compete. Restraint preserves hierarchy.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="principle-content">
      <h2>Statement</h2>
      <p>Restraint is what prevents meaning from collapsing under excess.</p>

      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        In systems where everything competes for attention, nothing receives it. Restraint is the deliberate refusal to add—a recognition that each additional element introduces cognitive cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not minimalism for aesthetic purposes. It is structural discipline: removing competing signals so that hierarchy becomes self-evident rather than constructed through visual weight.
      </p>

      <h2>Application</h2>
      <p>
        Restraint manifests in what is absent. Each refusal creates space for what remains to carry meaning without interference. When a system exercises restraint consistently, users learn to trust that what is present has purpose.
      </p>

      <h2>Citation</h2>
      <p class="citation-text">
        This principle may be cited as: <strong>I.1 (Restraint)</strong> or by plate reference <strong>E01-P03</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

